  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 400 AM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nCurrently skies were mostly clear across western and central ND\nwith a light southerly surface flow and temperatures in the \nupper 30s to mid 40s. \n\nA quasi-zonal upper level flow will persist over the local area\ntoday. A weak, dry cold front will propagate through the upper\nflow turning our southerly winds this morning to a northerly \nsurface flow, from west to east with the frontal passage. Even \nwith the cold front passage, temperatures are forecast to be \nsimilar (northwest ND) or warmer (southwest and central) than \nyesterday. We did utilize a blend of NBM and NBM90 guidance to \ntrend temperatures warmer. NBM ensemble guidance has been around\n3 to 7 degrees warmer than our observed temperatures since late\nlast week. \n\nWednesday through Friday upper level ridging builds over the \ncentral portion of the U.S. ahead of an upper level trough \ndeveloping in the eastern Pacific. Wednesday is forecast to be a\nlittle cooler than today as high pressure builds over the area \nbehind the cold front passage, but a southerly return flow is \nalready developing over western ND by Wednesday afternoon. This \nwill set the stage for a couple of unseasonably warm days on \nThursday and Friday. As mentioned in the previous discussion, \nnormal highs for this time of year are in the low to mid 50s, so\ntemperatures will be approaching 20 degrees above normal. If \nour current trend of cooler than observed forecast high \ntemperatures continues, which I suspect it may, Bismarck and \nDickinson could see near record highs Thursday and Friday.\n\nOn Friday, the aforementioned upper trough over the eastern\nPacific lifts into the Northern Rockies, with a surface low over\neastern Montana/western North Dakota by 00Z Saturday. With a\nsurge of warm advection ahead of the surface low, and with \nstrong shear lifting into the area, an isolated elevated \nthunderstorm can not be ruled out on Friday. The CSU machine \nlearning page indicated a threat for thunder on Friday. We'll \nsee how this trends through the week. Friday night into Saturday\nas the surface low lifts north into Canada, we see a surge of \ncold advection. Breezy southerly winds Friday shift west to \nnorthwest Friday night, with breezy to windy conditions expected\nFriday night, and Saturday as the upper level system winds up \nto our north. At this time it looks like mainly light \nprecipitation amounts from low to medium precipitation chances \nin North Dakota. The better precipitation amounts currently \ntrack from central Montana, northeast through Saskatchewan and \ninto northern Manitoba. We could see low chance for showers on \nFriday then possibly some wrap around precipitation clipping the\nnorthwest Friday night. \n\nGiven the warm temperatures and breezy conditions Friday, will\nneed to monitor fire weather concerns. Currently the return of\nmoisture with dew point temperatures climbing into the mid 50s\nis keeping minimum humidities above 30 percent over southwest\nND, with sustained southerly winds around 20 to 25 mph. Would\nnot be surprised to see some lower minRH values in the \nsouthwest Friday afternoon as short range guidance extends into\nFriday. Saturday will also be breezy to windy, but with much \ncooler temperatures. However, being in the dry sector of the \nsystem over Canada, we could see windy conditions combined with\nlow afternoon humidities.\n\nCooler temperatures remain Sunday and into the beginning of next\nweek with mostly dry conditions.",
